{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788351241,"nonce":"5YCmAPcrJhH0pnKjomi0W3p+p8U=","message":"**Resolved by investigation before dispatch, orchestrator, repair round 2026-09-02.** This issue asks for enclosure reporting without defining \"enclosed\", and a worker would have had to pick. Settled against the decompiled source; recorded in DESIGN.md's decisions log (commit 8c8680a).\n\n**1. The enclosure test is the game's own `ProperRoom`**, `Verse/Room.cs`:\n\n```csharp\npublic bool ProperRoom {\n  get {\n    if (TouchesMapEdge) return false;\n    for (int i = 0; i \u003c districts.Count; i++)\n      if (districts[i].RegionType == RegionType.Normal) return true;\n    return false;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAn unclosed layout leaks into the map-wide outdoor room, which touches the map edge, so `ProperRoom` is false. That is exactly what the run saw — `room-at` returned `outdoors: true` with `cells: 60082`, which is the whole outdoors and not a 60,000-cell freezer. Call the member and cite it; do not write a flood fill.\n\n**2. There are TWO failure modes here and the issue only names one.**\n\n```csharp\npublic bool UsesOutdoorTemperature =\u003e\n    TouchesMapEdge || OpenRoofCount \u003e= Mathf.CeilToInt(CellCount * 0.25f);\n```\n\nA room with a quarter of its roof missing is on **outdoor temperature with `ProperRoom` still true**. For a freezer — which is the entire point of this issue — that is the same dead colony by a different mechanism, and a check that reported only `ProperRoom` would have passed it clean. Report both, distinctly. (`PsychologicallyOutdoors` is a third reading with different thresholds, mood-facing, and is not the enclosure question — do not conflate the three.)\n\n**3. The layout-to-cells link already exists; no new IR field is needed.** `LayoutVerbs.Layouts.Open(...)` records a `CellRect` per placed layout, and it survives across days — the run called `construction {layout_id:\"ly-1\"}` successfully on day 40+. Intended interior cells are that rect minus the cells occupied by declared `Wall`/`Door` elements. Intended roofed cells are the IR's own `roof` mask, which every shipped template in `templates/` already carries.\n\n**4. \"Name the gap\" (acceptance item 2) is therefore a comparison, not a search.** A declared `Wall` or `Door` cell with no edifice standing — or carrying an unbuilt blueprint or frame — is the hole. A declared roofed cell that is unroofed is the thermal hole. Both come from data the mod already holds; no pathfinding.\n\nOne nuance that will bite an implementer: **a door does not break enclosure, an unbuilt door does.** The gap check keys on what stands at the cell, not on the declared def's type.","files":null}]}